\name{proteins_resolve_genes}
\alias{proteins_resolve_genes}
\title{Resolve every protein to stable Ensembl gene ids, against a gene set you pin}
\usage{
proteins_resolve_genes(databases, gtf = NULL, gene_set = NULL, xref = NULL,
  contaminants = NULL, threads = 1, on_error = "fail", timeout = NULL)
}
\arguments{
\item{databases}{The search database or databases, UniProt XML for real answers. A FASTA carries
  no gene links, so every FASTA protein is \code{not_in_source}; a caveat says so.}

\item{gtf}{An Ensembl GTF, plain or \code{.gz}. Only its \code{gene} rows are read. Give exactly one of
  \code{gtf} and \code{gene_set}.}

\item{gene_set}{Instead of \code{gtf}, a compact gene table written by mzLib's
  \code{EnsemblGeneSetWriter} (about 0.5 MB for human, against a 141 MB GTF). It carries the GTF's
  provenance, so rows are keyed exactly as against the GTF.}

\item{xref}{Optional: Ensembl's \code{Species.Assembly.Release.uniprot.tsv.gz}, for a second opinion.
  Each gene row then says whether Ensembl agrees, and a gene only Ensembl links gets its own row
  (\code{source == "ensembl_xref"}). Without it, \code{ensembl_xref_agrees} is \code{NA} - unknown, not false.}

\item{contaminants}{Databases to load as contaminants. Their proteins are
  \code{contaminant_not_mapped}: never mapped, and never silently dropped.}

\item{threads}{Databases read (and hashed) at once. Default 1; \code{-1} means every core. Same answer
  at any value.}

\item{on_error}{\code{"fail"} (the default) or \code{"skip"}, as for \code{\link{proteins_read}}.}

\item{timeout}{Seconds to allow, or \code{NULL} (the default) to wait: a gzipped GTF takes tens of
  seconds.
}

}
\description{
Resolve every protein in the given databases to stable Ensembl gene ids, counted against a caller-supplied, release-pinned gene set, with one outcome per protein and the hash of every input.

Wraps mzLib's \code{EnsemblGeneResolver}. The gene links come from the database itself (UniProt's
Ensembl \code{dbReference}s), and each is \strong{counted against the gene set you pass}: a gene in the set
counts; a gene outside it (an ALT haplotype, a patch) is dropped from \code{n_genes} but counted in
\code{off_primary_genes}, and a protein with only such genes is \code{off_primary_only}. Every protein gets
exactly one \code{outcome}: \code{resolved}, \code{multi_gene}, \code{off_primary_only}, \code{not_in_source},
\code{unrecognized_accession} or \code{contaminant_not_mapped}.
}
\details{
\strong{You supply the gene set, and you should pin it.} Gene ids, versions, symbols and the very set
of genes on the primary assembly change between Ensembl releases, so a resolution means something
only relative to one release. Use Ensembl's \strong{primary-assembly} GTF
(\code{Species.Assembly.Release.gtf.gz}, not the \code{chr_patch_hapl_scaff} one), keep Ensembl's file name
so the release is recorded, and keep \code{gene_set$sha256} with your results. Nothing is downloaded
or defaulted here.
}
\value{
An \code{mzlibr_gene_resolutions}. \code{file_count}, \code{read_count} and \code{failed_count} count
  database files; \code{protein_count} counts the proteins resolved; \code{record_count} counts the rows of
  \code{resolutions} - at least one per protein, one per gene for \code{multi_gene}, plus any
  \code{ensembl_xref} rows. \code{outcome_counts} is a named vector: outcome to number of proteins, every
  outcome present, zeros included. \code{gene_set} is a list (\code{source_file_name}, \code{sha256},
  \code{release}, \code{genome_build}, \code{genebuild_last_updated}, \code{gene_count}), and \code{xref} one like it, or
  \code{NULL} without \code{xref}. \code{files} has one row per input, with its \code{search_database_sha256} (of
  the decompressed database).

  \code{resolutions} is a data.frame whose columns after \code{source_index} (1-based) and \code{source_path}
  are mzLib's own \code{GeneResolutionTsv} schema. \code{n_genes} counts genes on the gene set's assembly;
  \code{off_primary_genes} counts linked genes outside it. \code{gene_id}, \code{versioned_gene_id} and
  \code{gene_biotype} are \code{NA} when the row has no gene; \code{isoform} is \code{NA} without a \code{-N} suffix.

}
\section{One row per gene, never a pick}{


A \code{multi_gene} protein has one row per gene, and a protein with no gene one outcome row. No cell
joins several genes, and nothing chooses among them for you.

}
